# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# mica-bridge.service — Stage 2, infrastructure half only
#
# NOT INSTALLED BY THIS FILE. Installing it (and its first start) is the
# arbiter's act, by hand, as root, AFTER `relay-bridge.sh` has laid
# `/opt/mica-bridge` (see that script; it is what actually populates
# /opt/mica-bridge/mica-bridge/*):
#
#   sudo install -m 0644 -o root -g root \
#     deploy/mica-bridge.service /etc/systemd/system/mica-bridge.service
#   sudo systemctl daemon-reload
#   sudo systemctl start mica-bridge
#   sudo -u mica-bridge /usr/bin/node \
#     /opt/mica-machine/node_modules/ts-node/dist/bin.js \
#     -P /opt/mica-bridge/mica-bridge/tsconfig.json \
#     /opt/mica-bridge/mica-bridge/mica-service.ts --check   # read the banner first
#
# ── RUNTIME CUSTODY (Stage 0 verifier F1) ────────────────────────────────────
#
# ExecStart below names an ABSOLUTE Node binary, never `#!/usr/bin/env node`
# resolution: this box's system PATH gives `/usr/bin/node`, and `readlink -f
# /usr/bin/node` on this box resolves to ITSELF (a real ELF binary, not a
# symlink into nvm) at v18.19.1 — verified this session. The builder's own
# interactive shell runs nvm's v22.22.0, which a systemd unit never sees
# (units do not source a user's shell start files or nvm's shell init). Using `env node` here
# would silently run a DIFFERENT Node than whatever the receipt/verifier
# tested against. The Stage 2 receipt records both versions and this
# ExecStart's absolute paths so the unit and the verifier are provably the
# same interpreter.
#
# ExecStart's ts-node entrypoint is mica-machine's own pinned dependency
# (`/opt/mica-machine/node_modules/ts-node/dist/bin.js`, verified to
# exist this session) — NOT a citadel-local node_modules, because citadel
# has none (Stage 0's own finding, unchanged). This means /opt/mica-machine
# must be laid (relayed) BEFORE /opt/mica-bridge starts; that ordering is
# named, not enforced by this file (RequiresMountsFor names a state
# directory, not a sibling release tree — see the header's note above).
#
# `mica-service.ts` uses only Node core (`http`, `fs`, `path`, `crypto`
# transitively via canonical-json.ts) and no syntax requiring Node >= 20 —
# `URL`, optional chaining, and nullish coalescing are all available since
# Node 14. It runs correctly under v18.19.1 (exercised directly this
# session, receipt records the command).
#
# ── RULING K, AMENDED (the arbiter, 2026-09-18 ~20:5x, "Ruling (b) it is") ────────
#
# There is NO single canonical socket for this service. `mica-bridge.socket`
# (a systemd `.socket` unit) is RETIRED — deleted from this directory, not
# merely unused. Peer identity is per-caller Unix sockets under filesystem
# permissions: the service itself creates and binds one socket per declared
# `routes[]` uid, at `/run/mica-bridge/peers/<uid>/guard.sock`, and identifies
# the peer by WHICH of those sockets accepted the connection — never a
# queried credential (Node core cannot read `SO_PEERCRED`; a native addon was
# declined). See `peer-auth.ts`'s header for the mechanism and its one stated
# residual (root can connect to any of them; X10 forbids a uid-0 route).
#
# `RuntimeDirectory=mica-bridge` below is the PARENT of every per-uid socket
# directory (`/run/mica-bridge/`, recreated on every boot since `/run` is
# tmpfs) — it is not a socket-activation directory and no `.socket` unit
# points at it. The per-uid subdirectories THEMSELVES
# (`/run/mica-bridge/peers/<uid>/`, each owned `<uid>:mica-bridge` mode 0770)
# are recreated on every boot by `deploy/mica-bridge.tmpfiles.conf`
# (`systemd-tmpfiles`, installed separately — see that file's header) because
# this service's own `User=mica-bridge` has no privilege to chown a directory
# to a different uid; tmpfiles.d runs as root before this unit starts.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

[Unit]
Description=MICA bridge service (per-caller Unix sockets; /guard evaluates through the machine)
Documentation=file:///opt/mica-bridge/mica-bridge/mica-service.ts
RequiresMountsFor=/var/lib/mica-bridge

[Service]
Type=simple
User=mica-bridge
Group=mica-bridge

WorkingDirectory=/opt/mica-bridge

# The pinned in-tree ts-node binary, DIRECTLY, never npx — the same idiom as
# mica-proxy.service / mica-witnessd.service (the machine tree's deploy/). The
# absolute Node binary is /usr/bin/node (see header); the ts-node entrypoint
# is mica-machine's dependency, not citadel's (citadel has no node_modules).
ExecStart=/usr/bin/node /opt/mica-machine/node_modules/ts-node/dist/bin.js -P /opt/mica-bridge/mica-bridge/tsconfig.json /opt/mica-bridge/mica-bridge/mica-service.ts

Environment=MICA_BRIDGE_DECLARATIONS=/opt/mica-bridge/mica-bridge/declarations.config.json
Environment=MICA_BRIDGE_SOCKET_DIR=/run/mica-bridge

ExecReload=/bin/kill -HUP $MAINPID
Restart=on-failure

NoNewPrivileges=yes
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=yes
StateDirectory=mica-bridge
#
# NO RuntimeDirectory= — DELIBERATELY REMOVED (S136, 2026-09-20, ruled by the arbiter
# at the terminal during Stage 4 / F-4, before this unit had ever started once).
#
# This line used to read `RuntimeDirectory=mica-bridge`. On systemd 255 (this
# box, read against its own systemd.exec(5) this session) that directive does
# two things this design cannot survive:
#
#   1. "If the specified directories already exist and their owning user or
#      group do not match the configured ones, ALL FILES AND DIRECTORIES BELOW
#      the specified directories as well as the directories themselves will
#      have their file ownership RECURSIVELY CHANGED to match what is
#      configured."  /run/mica-bridge is root:root (tmpfiles.d); this unit runs
#      as mica-bridge:mica-bridge. They do not match. So the FIRST start would
#      have recursively chowned peers/980 (mica-housekeeper), peers/981
#      (mica-alerter) and peers/1000 (the operator account) to one single owner —
#      DISSOLVING RULING K. Peer identity IS the ownership of peers/<uid>: the
#      door you could reach, not the claim you made. One owner, no doors. And
#      /status would have gone on reporting guardBound: true throughout.
#
#   2. RuntimeDirectoryPreserve= defaults to `no`: "the directories specified
#      in RuntimeDirectory= are ALWAYS REMOVED when the service stops." The
#      first stop — or the restart at the end of relay-bridge.sh — would have
#      deleted the whole peer tree, which this service, being non-root, can
#      never rebuild with the correct per-uid owners.
#
# Neither is reachable by any test in this repo: every test runs in a temp tree
# the tester owns, where a recursive chown to the tester changes nothing
# observable. mica-service.test.ts:1407 even models
# {path: "/run/mica-bridge", owner: "mica-bridge", group: "mica-bridge"} as a
# FAILURE case — it asserts against the tmpfiles DECLARATION, while the unit
# would have produced exactly that state at RUNTIME. The test had the right
# property and the wrong artifact.
#
# The per-uid peer socket subdirectories (/run/mica-bridge/peers/<uid>/) and
# their parents are created before this unit starts by
# deploy/mica-bridge.tmpfiles.conf via systemd-tmpfiles, run as root — see the
# header above ("RULING K, AMENDED") and that file's own header. That file is
# now the SOLE authority on this tree; systemd must not second-guess it.
#
# ReadWritePaths=/run/mica-bridge below is what RuntimeDirectory= was silently
# ALSO providing: ProtectSystem=strict mounts "the entire file system hierarchy
# read-only, except for the API file system subtrees /dev/, /proc/ and /sys/",
# so without that path named here the service could not bind its sockets at
# all. It grants the NAMESPACE write only; DAC still applies, so mica-bridge
# can bind inside peers/<uid> (group mica-bridge, 0770) but CANNOT create new
# peer directories under peers/ (0711 root:root). That asymmetry is the point:
# root decides which doors exist, the service only answers at them.
#
# mica-service.ts does not read $RUNTIME_DIRECTORY; it resolves the socket root
# itself (mica-service.ts:1241). Nothing else depended on the directive.
ReadWritePaths=/var/lib/mica-bridge /run/mica-bridge
PrivateTmp=yes

UMask=0027

# NO [Install] SECTION (Amendment 3 / verifier F6). The brief requires none
# for this unit — the arbiter installs and starts it by hand each time, not via
# `systemctl enable`. A prior version of this file carried
# `[Install]\nWantedBy=multi-user.target`, found and removed at the
# verifier's gate; it was never ruled and should not have been here.
